- How to manage books for reading is in [Buy, E-books, Used-all]
- Format that you used for reading books is "Both"

---

These conditions are consistent with the example given and general knowledge about undergraduate students' reading habits.

---DIVIDER---

Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Gender: Likely no strong direct causal relationship with reading academic books most of the time.
- Age: Older undergraduates might have more academic reading due to advanced coursework.
- Department: Students in academic or technical departments (e.g., CSE, SWE, Pharmacy, English) are more likely to read academic books than others.
- Amount of time spent reading books per day: More hours likely indicates reading academic books most of the time.
- Reason for reading books: "Academic Purpose" strongly correlates with reading academic books most of the time; other reasons less so.
- How to manage books for reading: Methods like "Borrow," "Buy," or "E-books" might indicate active reading habits; "Used-all" or "Others" less clear.
- Number of e-books read last two years: Higher numbers suggest more academic reading if the books are academic in nature.
- Number of printed books read last two years: Similarly, higher numbers suggest more academic reading.
- Favorite books for leisure time: If leisure reading is mostly "Non-Fiction" or "Biography," it may indicate an academic inclination; "Fiction" or "Politics" less so.
- What is the change felt after reading books: "Increase Academic Performance" or "Increase Problem Solving Skill" indicates academic reading.
- Who influenced you to read books: "Self-Decision" might correlate with more serious reading habits.
- Which language books do you like to read: English might correlate with academic reading in many countries.
- Where do you read printed books: Library or Home might indicate academic reading environments.
- Do you read Newspaper: No or Yes may not strongly correlate.
- The format used for reading books: Printed or E-books may not directly correlate, but frequent use of E-books may indicate academic reading.
- Frequency of reading books: "On everyday basis" or "A few times a week" indicates more academic reading than "A few times a month" or less.

---

Step 2. Based on Step 1 and the example, 10 different conditions per answer class:

10 different conditions for class "0" (Does NOT read academic books most of the time):
- Reason for reading books is in [Entertainment Purpose, To increase my English vocabulary knowledge]
- Amount of time spent reading books per day < 1
- Frequency of reading books is in [A few times a month, Rarely]
- Number of printed books you read last two years is in [1-5, nan]
- Number of e-books you read last two years is in [1-5, nan]
- Department is in [BBA, NFE, Textile, Civil, JMC, Others]
- Favorite books for reading to spend leisure time is in [Fiction, Politics, Others]
- What is the change that you felt after reading books is not in [Increase Academic Performance, Increase Problem Solving Skill]
- How to manage books for reading is in [Others, Used-all]
- Frequency of reading books is <= 1 time per week (implied by categorical frequency)

10 different conditions for class "1" (Reads academic books most of the time):
- Reason for reading books is Academic Purpose
- Amount of time spent reading books per day >= 2
- Frequency of reading books is in [On everyday basis, A few times a week]
- Number of printed books you read last two years is in [6-10, 11-15, 16-20, More than 20]
- Number of e-books you read last two years is in [6-10, 11-15, 16-20, More than 20]
- Department is in [CSE, SWE, English, Pharmacy, EEE, CIS, MCT]
- Favorite books for reading to spend leisure time is in [Non-Fiction, Biography]
- What is the change that you felt after reading books is in [Increase Academic Performance, Increase Problem Solving Skill]
- How to manage books for reading is in [Buy, Borrow, E-books]
- Age is within range [20, 25]

